IBM testified in the Microsoft trial that Microsoft threathened to remove the
right to sell Windows if IBM continues to sell its own products.


Now, we also know that Bobby Palmer agreed to sabotage many of DEC's products
(notably messaging) and adopt MS's products instead in exchange for the right
to continue to live. This is very similar to what has happened to IBM.

I personally would think it very important that those judging this think not
see the IBM case as isolated and realise that many companies were put into
this situation.

I doubt very much that Compaq would have the guts to testify, risking to get
Billy mad and have a tampertantrum.

However, how about getting DECUS to testify ?

Think about it. It would clear Compaq from any responsability so MS couldn't
do anything against Compaq, and since DECUS will be "destroyed" when the new
group is formed, there could not be any long term harm. It would actually be
in Compaq's advantage to use DECUS to do the dirty work because it would then
make it easier for Compaq to push its products which are seen as competing
against MS.
(eg: all software that Digital/Tandem had). And Compaq could deny any
"involvement" should MS get mad at Compaq because of that appearance.

What do you think ?



