  Please take a look at the OpenVMS Ask The Wizard area:

    http://www.openvms.digital.com/wizard/

  Specifically looking for the shareable image cookbook document, and 
  at one of the Wizard questions on the topic of using COMMONs in C: 
  "Problems with data reversion in COMMON?"  (The latter will eventually
  find its way into the cookbook or into a similar document.)

  And see if these two will help you any...

  Other considerations involve how C is resolving the Fortran symbols
  (or not, in this case) located in the COMMON, and if the code correctly
  contends with the differing page sizes on OpenVMS Alpha systems -- on
  Alpha, pages can be from 8KB to 64KB in size, while VAX pages are 0.5KB.
  You may end up becoming more familar with the Alpha SOLITARY attribute 
  in the linker options file... :-)

  If you want to pursue this, I'd recommend starting with something like
  the example in "Problems with data reversion in COMMON?" and with your
  Fortran code and putting together a reproduer we can look at...  (The
  example is a C executable image and a C shareable image that both
  reference data stored in a C COMMON.)

  I personally really dislike using COMMONs and position-dependence in
  general -- they do have their uses, but I much prefer to use global 
  sections as I've encountered various maintenance headaches with COMMONs 
  over the years.

 -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
 Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




